hey Luke here at the outdoor boys  YouTube channel and this is my forging  setup and I've done a lot of projects  and videos with this Forge I built an  african spear japanese pocket knife  couple axes so i get a lot of questions  from people who are interested in having  something similar so if you want a  really cheap forge setup this is the  video for you I'm going to show you  exactly how to do it so this is an old  video I posted on my other YouTube  channel the catfish and carp YouTube  channel so I reacted it and just cleaned  it up a little bit added a little bit of  extra footage and here you go here's how  to build a fort on a budget  all right so let me give you kind of a  rundown of what I'm gonna be building  let me show you the design now this  furnace is a wood-burning furnace it  runs off of charcoal or wood scraps you  can also use coal if you want and it is  powered by a shop vac so the shop vac is  blowing air into the furnace you can  also use a hairdryer you could use a  hand  blower specialty blacksmithing blowers  but this is what I had around the house  and it works really good let me give you  a little example here  now I'm using lumber scraps in wood but  if you use coal I hear that it gets even  hotter and it works a little bit better  you don't have to stoke it as often and  it burns a little bit hotter so if you  can source coal and use that instead  that's a great option the great thing  about these wood-burning forges is you  can use just about anything to make them  I use sheet steel here but you know I  could have used this helium canister cut  it in two and use it you can use the a  BBQ shell you can you could use a metal  trash can probably I mean in just about  anything will work if you insulate it  well enough the thing is is that sand is  a fabulous insulator and so as long as  you have a good layer of sand on top of  the steel and the fires high up you  shouldn't have any problem there is  almost zero heat being transferred down  so as long as the fire rides high up on  the furnace the metal doesn't get heated  up this is cold okay  everything but the very rim of the metal  is cold to the touch and then you  basically have some sort of base to put  it on and I've got a frame made out of  two by fours and I've got a little work  area here with a vise okay so the first  step in my Forge project is I'm going to  put holes in this pipe now this is a  two-inch heavy gauge black steel pipe  there you go six holes  well I may be the worst welder ever and  this may be the worst welding machine  ever but we actually did it I think this  will actually hold up oh it's so on yeah  I don't even want to show you your  close-up  I'll tell you what that test run works  pretty good it fired right up and a lot  of air going through there even with the  sand covering up the vents it's cold  down here where the sand is any part  that's covered with sand is good you  know it's a little lopsided so this part  is exposed there was no sand and I can  see where it's warped and bent a little  bit I don't think it's causing any  permanent damage to it but I think what  I need to do is once it cools down I  need to level it out and make sure that  the metals not not exposed that all the  metals covered with sand and I think  we're ready to go now I just need to  mount my anvil and we'll go from there I  think this might do the trick  well it's a bit low and it's uneven on  one end so you'd have to term it he's  worried about there  I don't know if I made it better or  worse  half a bubble off one okay for fuel I'm  using charcoal and I've got charcoal  that's just leftover from my fireplace  and from forging the night before and  you can buy or make charcoal comes in  these chunks but you want it big chunky  stuff we want hardwood charcoal or you  can get pieces of wood so just you want  chunks little chunks like this you know  scraps from this and that these old  two-by-four chunks work really well I  like these best just about this length  yeah used up a little more than half the  charcoal and half the pile quite a bit  of fuel of course I'm not very good at  this  and so you know if I had to do it over  again I think I could have done it much  quicker and used but much less fuel but  you know  it's been over a year since I made this  Forge  I've been using it quite heavily and let  me show you how well it's held up and  kind of some of the pros and cons first  off the big thing is I'm using a wooden  base and you can see here there's some  bird marks  we're occasionally hot coals will fall  off the side and get in these cracks and  burn and you can see you can see here  and maybe down here a few places that  it's burned the wood this side not so  bad because usually I'm working on this  side I can see it happen and I could  just brush the coal off so what I've  done to keep that from happening is when  I build a fire I take one log and just  put it right there and I take another  log put it right there and I put my  coals my fire in between and that keeps  coals from spilling over a bigger  problem with this design is that it  Wiggles a lot you can see here there's  quite a bit of play and so when you've  got something in the vise and you're  sawing on it or whatever this thing  shakes and shimmy is quite a bit and the  more shimmies the the looser it gets so  occasionally I have to go through and  put nails back in and since I did this  video I added these cross pieces here  and here and over on this side and down  there to try to give it a little bit  more rigidity and I really need  add some more so if you're doing this  design at home and you want this thing  to hold up well under for many years use  exterior deck screws and add more cross  pieces another thing I have done  differently is in the video you saw me  use sand and gravel to make the forge  wouldn't do that  just gravel don't even monkey with the  sand now let me show you the anvil the  anvil here is held up pretty well for a  $65 anvil it's it's surprisingly soft  steel and so there's little dents in it  and from where you know you miss a good  chunk of these dents are from where I  let the Boy Scouts come and work on my  my anvil and a lot of them just missed  so much that they they just swing as  hard as they could without much accuracy  and so a lot of these dents are from Boy  Scouts but all in all for being a really  cheap set up that I did myself without a  lot of prior experience it's held up  really well to a lot of use I've used  this thing a bunch and I'm pretty proud  of it so it's a design that definitely  can be approved upon and if you have  better materials and better tools and  better skills go for it but this'll work  if you're looking to just get into the  supplier to the Hobby this will do it  thanks for watching we hope you guys  enjoyed this video if you'd like to see  more great videos from the outdoor boys  YouTube channel don't forget to click  Subscribe we put out new videos every  week and don't forget to hit that Bell  button so you'll get notified when we  put out another great video

